<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tawi-Tawi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tawi-Tawi is the southernmost province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, a group of islands at the south-western end of the Sulu archipelago, close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. Its main island is Tawi-Tawi and its capital Bongao. Part of the Sulu Sultanate's domain, it includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simunul">Simunul</a>, where tradition places the first mosque in the Philippines.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tawi-tawi.jpg" alt="Tawi-Tawi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ervin Malicdem · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panguan_Island.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tawi-Tawi is the southernmost province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulu-archipelago">Sulu Archipelago</a> between the Sulu Sea in the north and the Celebes Sea in the south. It shares sea borders with the Malaysian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">Sabah</a> and the Indonesian province of North Kalimantan, both on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. It consists of Tawitawi Island and 106 surrounding islands and islets with a land area of 1,087.4 square kilometres, and the main island accounts for 580.5 of them. The name is the Sinama form of jawi-jawi, Malay for the banyan tree, which grows abundantly there. The capital is Bongao, and the island supports several endemic species, among them the Tawitawi brown dove and the Sulu hornbill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Carbon dating of bones, jars and shells from the Bolobok Rock Shelter places human presence there between 6,810 BCE and 3,190 BCE, among the earliest evidence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. For centuries the area was a centre of Sama culture, and some islands hold old burial grounds with Sama okil carvings of ancestors. The first mosque in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, the Sheik Karimol Makhdum Mosque, was established in 1380 by the Arab trader and missionary Makhdum Karim. The area was heavily converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, especially after the founding of the Sultanate of Sulu, although many Sama kept part of their older animist religions.</p><p>Later centuries brought foreign claims. The sultan of Sulu made agreements with Alexander Dalrymple of the British East India Company in 1761 and 1762, and in 1878 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-von-overbeck">Baron von Overbeck</a> leased the sultan's land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-north-borneo-company">British North Borneo Company</a>. The Spanish built a naval base at Tata'an and garrisons at Bongao and Siasi. American forces occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolo">Jolo</a> in 1899, and by the Treaty of Washington in 1900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded Sibutu and Cagayan de Sulu to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> for US$100,000. The Imperial Japanese Navy used the waters as a fleet anchorage during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The municipalities of Tawi-Tawi were part of Sulu until 11 September 1973, when Presidential Decree No. 302 made it a province, with Romulo Espaldon as its first governor. Its seat was at first Bato-Bato, in the municipality of Balimbing, and moved to Bongao in 1979. The province lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangsamoro">Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao</a> and has 11 municipalities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tawi-Tawi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Islam probably reached the islands with Muslim traders and teachers in the fourteenth century; Sulu tradition remembers Karim ul-Makhdum, credited with building a mosque on Simunul in <b>Tawi-Tawi</b>, and Raja Baguinda, a prince said to have come from Minangkabau in Sumatra.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
